.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt BIND 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bind
.Nd assign a socket's local port or interface
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.Ft int
.Fn bind "int fd" "const struct sockaddr *addr" "socklen_t addrlen"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn bind
sets the local port or interface of the socket
.Fa fd
from the address at
.Fa addr .
.Fa addrlen
is the address length in bytes.
The kernel interface is
.Xr socketcall 2
operation 2.
.Ss IPv4 sockets
For TCP and UDP, the port is read in network byte order from a
.Vt sockaddr_in .
At least eight address bytes are required.
The address and family fields are not checked for these socket types;
binding does not restrict a socket to an IPv4 address.
A nonzero port below 1024 requires effective user ID 0.
.Pp
A UDP bind registers the port for receiving datagrams.
Port zero selects a free port from 49152 through 65535.
Two sockets may share a port only when all sockets using it have
.Dv SO_REUSEADDR
set and the binding owners have the same effective user ID.
Rebinding first removes the socket's previous UDP registration, even
when the new registration fails.
.Pp
A TCP bind records the port.
It rejects a nonzero port that already has a live listener, but does
not reserve the port against another bind before
.Xr listen 2 .
Binding TCP port zero leaves port selection to the later operation.
.Pp
There is no loopback interface.
At the socket boundary, a destination in
.Ql 127/8
is an alias for the current primary local address.
Address-reporting calls return that canonical address.
.Pp
A raw ICMP socket accepts address zero, the primary local address, or
an address in
.Ql 127/8 .
Another address gives
.Er EADDRNOTAVAIL .
The raw socket used only for interface queries rejects binding.
.Ss Packet sockets
An
.Dv AF_PACKET
socket takes a 20-byte
.Vt sockaddr_ll .
Its interface index may be zero for all interfaces or the index of an
existing interface.
The protocol, in network byte order, may select IPv4 or ARP; zero keeps
the socket's existing protocol.
Changing the interface or protocol discards queued packets.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn bind
returns 0 on success.
On failure it returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
The raw kernel call returns a negative error number on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default-suite probe
.Pa tests/loopprobe.c
binds a UDP socket to address zero and port 7100, then sends a datagram
to
.Ql 127.0.0.1:7100
and reads it back.
It also checks that two TCP binds can precede a listener, but a bind
to an existing listener's port fails with
.Er EADDRINUSE .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EADDRNOTAVAIL
.It Er EACCES
A caller without effective user ID 0 requested a nonzero port below 1024.
.It Er EADDRINUSE
A UDP port cannot be shared, no ephemeral port is available, or memory
for a UDP binding cannot be allocated.
A TCP port already has a live listener.
.It Er EADDRNOTAVAIL
A raw ICMP bind requests an address other than zero, the primary local
address, or an address in
.Ql 127/8 .
.It Er EFAULT
The socketcall argument array or the required address bytes cannot be read.
.It Er EINVAL
The address length is negative or less than eight bytes, a packet
address is shorter than 20 bytes, or a packet protocol is neither zero,
IPv4 nor ARP.
.It Er ENOBUFS
Memory for a packet-socket binding cannot be allocated.
.It Er ENODEV
The packet address names an interface index other than zero or an
existing interface.
.It Er ENOTSOCK
.Fa fd
is invalid or does not refer to a socket.
.It Er EOPNOTSUPP
The socket is the raw descriptor used only for interface queries.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr accept 2 ,
.Xr connect 2 ,
.Xr getsockname 2 ,
.Xr listen 2 ,
.Xr setsockopt 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr socketcall 2
